% Define the variables for the coordinates of the squares
array[int] of var int: x_coords;
array[int] of var int: y_coords;

% Initialize the variables
x_coords = [var 0..base | i in 1..num_squares];
y_coords = [var 0..base | i in 1..num_squares];

% Define the constraints
constraint forall(i in 1..num_squares) (
    x_coords[i] + sides[i] <= base
);
constraint forall(i in 1..num_squares) (
    y_coords[i] + sides[i] <= base
);

% Non-overlapping constraint
constraint forall(i in 1..num_squares, j in i+1..num_squares) (
    (x_coords[i] + sides[i] <= x_coords[j]) \/
    (x_coords[j] + sides[j] <= x_coords[i]) \/
    (y_coords[i] + sides[i] <= y_coords[j]) \/
    (y_coords[j] + sides[j] <= y_coords[i])
);

% Surface constraint
constraint sum([sides[i]*sides[i] | i in 1..num_squares]) = base*base;

% Search for a solution
solve satisfy;

% Print the coordinates of the squares
output [
    "{",
    "\"x_coords\": [",
    join(", ", [show(x) | x in x_coords]),
    "],",
    "\"y_coords\": [",
    join(", ", [show(y) | y in y_coords]),
    "]",
    "}"
];
```

This model uses the standard Minizinc library and global constraints to solve the perfect square placement problem. The `x_coords` and `y_coords` variables represent the coordinates of the squares, and the constraints ensure that the squares do not overlap and that the surface of the packing square is equal to the sum of the surfaces of the smaller squares. The `solve satisfy` statement searches for a solution that satisfies all the constraints, and the `output` statement prints the coordinates of the squares in JSON format.

